  "summary": "Fresh independent inspection found all seven deterministic hard gates and all four quality targets passing. The 106 knowledge-lifecycle tests, public-safety gate, citation consistency check, and 10 citation tests also passed. Captured, researching, corroborated, public-ready, projected, rejected, and superseded records were inspected. The NYC Artist Coalition answer correctly separates direct public evidence from governance interpretation: the Council transcript, CreateNYC records, coalition tools and testimony, and Council advisory-board record establish reciprocal feedback, organized recommendations, constituent-facing policy translation, repeat testimony, stakeholder expertise, and coalition representation. The civic-cultural-intermediary explanation is explicitly labeled an inference; Finkelpearl's complete private motive remains unknown. Espinal retains credit for legislative authority, Olympia Kazi for testimony and her board appointment, and other participants for collective work. Jamie's event and participation-system role is separately linked to direct-role evidence and bounded against sole authorship. Government recognition, interaction, advisory representation, and campaign activity are not converted into policy adoption or causation. The two accomplishment claims remain public-ready but deferred, while the two governance explanations remain corroborated and unprojected. External spot checks of the [Council transcript](https://legistar.council.nyc.gov/View.ashx?GUID=68A2E207-53EA-43CA-A03E-F879A113464E&ID=5271559&M=F), [CreateNYC record](https://createnyc.cityofnewyork.us/the-cultural-plan/issue-areas/nyc-artsts/), [Council advisory-board announcement](https://council.nyc.gov/press/2018/07/12/1624/), and [coalition testimony](https://nycartc.com/nyc-artist-coalition-repeal-the-cabaret-law-bill-1652-testimony/) matched the canonical propositions. All human gates remain pending."
